# PROPOSED, NOT APPLIED -- contracts are signed by a person, not by a tool.
#
# Adds `effect_class` beside each script's existing `effect` sentence, so the
# MCP server can decide whether a role may run it. The prose stays: "temporarily
# muxes P9_17/18/21 as GPIO then restores exact state" says more than any enum,
# and cannot be enforced. This is the machine-readable half beside it, not a
# replacement.
#
# Until this is signed, every gate9 script counts as `mutate` and a server
# launched `--role measurement` can run none of them. That is the intended
# state: failing closed means a script nobody has classified is a script nobody
# has thought about.
#
# The classification:
#
#   read    diagnose_rtase, mcspi_kernel_state
#   mutate  mcspi_ch0conf, spi_loopback_mode_compare, spi_clocked_response_verify,
#           p9_21_gpio_loopback_probe, mcspi_loopback_selftest
#
# Five of seven are `mutate` because **restoring a setting afterwards does not
# make a script a read.** The restore is a second change rather than the absence
# of a first one, and a process that dies between them leaves the board altered.
# Three of those five say exactly that in their own `effect` line.
#
# Reviewer: the call worth checking is `mcspi_ch0conf`. Its description says it
# reads controller registers; its effect line adds "runs existing loopback
# traffic", which drives the bus, and that is why it is `mutate` rather than
# `read`. If that traffic is genuinely inert, reclassifying it would give
# measurement a third readable diagnostic.
#
# Unrelated and still open: this contract has a `pinned_file_drift` finding on
# spi_clocked_response_verify (disk 735c91d3..., contract 25589ab5...). This
# patch does not touch that hash. Settle it separately, after reading the diff.
#
# The file has mixed line endings on disk -- 157 CRLF lines and 44 LF -- so a
# plain `git apply` refuses it. That is a property of the contract, not of this
# patch, and normalising the whole file would be an unrequested change to a
# signed document. Use --ignore-whitespace, or fix the endings deliberately in
# a commit of their own first.
#
# To apply, after review:
#   git apply --ignore-whitespace QKD_PROJ/contracts/proposals/2026-08-21-gate9-effect-class.patch
# To see it first:
#   git apply --check --ignore-whitespace --stat <the same path>
#
